<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM fairness should be evaluated through in-situ conversational behavior rather than standardized-test Q&amp;A benchmarks. We show that the standardized-test paradigm can be structurally unreliable: surface-level prompt construction choices, although entirely orthogonal to the fairness question being tested, account for the majority of score variance, shift fairness conclusions in both the direction and the magnitude, and result in severe discordance in model rankings. We develop MAC-Fairness, a multi-agent conversational framework that embeds controlled variation factors into multi-round dialogue for in-situ behavior evaluation, examining how models’ conversational behavior shifts when identity is varied as part of natural multi-agent interaction. Repurposing standardized-test questions as conversation seeds rather than as the evaluation instrument, we evaluate position persistence (how they hold positions, from the self-perspective) and peer receptiveness (how receptive they are to peers, from the other-perspective) across 8 million conversation transcripts spanning multiple models and identity presence configurations. In-situ behavioral evaluation reveals stable, model-specific behavioral signatures that could generalize across benchmarks differing in fairness targets and evaluation methodologies, a form of evidence the standardized-test paradigm does not offer.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id4" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,
<br class="ltx_break">Not Standardized-Test Scores</span></p>
<table id="p1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Equal contribution   
Correspondence to: Zeyu Tang <a href="mailto:zeyu@cs.stanford.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;zeyu@cs.stanford.edu&gt;</a> and Sanmi Koyejo <a href="mailto:sanmi@cs.stanford.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;sanmi@cs.stanford.edu&gt;</a>.
We provide implementation together with comprehensive documentation in the GitHub repository <a href="https://github.com/stair-lab/mac-fairness" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/stair-lab/mac-fairness</a>.
</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fairness evaluation of large language models (LLMs) has converged on a standard methodology: probe the model with questions referencing sensitive attributes such as race, gender, and age, and compare models by the resulting aggregate bias scores <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nangia et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tamkin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Röttger et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Recent work has advocated for benchmark suites that move away from relying on any single benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, yet the analysis still compares a set of per-benchmark scores without surfacing behavioral patterns that are both statistically significant and shared across benchmarks.
The literature has also extended evaluation from single-turn to multi-turn dialogue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, tracking bias across longer interactions across stages including context understanding, user interaction, and instruction trade-offs.
However, the model remains a test-taker whose individual responses are classified as biased or unbiased.
Whether the protocol is single-benchmark or multi-benchmark-suite, single-turn or multi-turn, the implicit assumption is that a fixed question-answer protocol produces stable, interpretable measurements of fairness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is fragile.
We show that the standardized-test paradigm suffers from a structural instability that undermines its reliability as a fairness measurement instrument.
Surface-level prompt construction choices that are entirely orthogonal to the fairness question being tested, such as how answer choices are formatted or whether the model is asked to state its answer before or after its rationale, account for the majority of score variance, even after repeated runs to account for decoding stochasticity.
Bias scores swing in both the direction and the magnitude across these choices, and model rankings exhibit severe discordance.
The problem is not that any single prompt format makes a model more likely to produce biased responses, but that the standardized-test fairness evaluation paradigm provides no mechanism for distinguishing signal from measurement artifact, risking <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fair on the sheet but biased in the street</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Distinct from previous research, our work uses multi-agent conversation as a behavioral evaluation harness: the conversation itself is the measurement instrument, and identity variation within natural dialogue is the experimental manipulation.
Specifically, we develop MAC-Fairness, a multi-agent conversational framework that repurposes standardized-test questions as conversation seeds rather than as the evaluation instrument.
Across 8 million transcripts spanning multiple models, benchmarks, and conversation configurations, we find stable, model-specific behavioral signatures that replicate across benchmarks differing in fairness targets and evaluation methodologies.
Our contributions are threefold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that the standardized-test paradigm for LLM fairness evaluation is structurally unreliable: prompt construction choices dominate score variance, reverse fairness conclusions, and destabilize model rankings.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We develop MAC-Fairness, a multi-agent conversational framework that operationalizes fairness evaluation as in-situ behavioral measurement rather than question answering.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify stable, cross-benchmark behavioral signatures, in both self-perspective position persistence and other-perspective peer receptiveness, that reveal model-specific patterns of identity sensitivity independent of benchmark-specific metrics.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM Fairness Evaluation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fairness evaluation in LLMs has evolved from embedding-level measures and controlled template-based tests to broader assessments across tasks and social groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nangia et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tamkin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Röttger et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Recent work extends fairness evaluation to multi-turn dialogue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, yet the dominant paradigm remains standardized-test Q&amp;A in which model’s answer to the question is evaluation instrument.
Rather than measuring what models say in response to fairness-probing questions, we aim to measure how models behave in multi-agent conversational dynamics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-Agent Interaction in LLMs</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-agent LLM frameworks have been developed primarily to improve task performance through structured collaboration.
For instance, differentiated roles can coordinate software development <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, debate-based protocols aggregate diverse model outputs to boost reasoning accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Estornell &amp; Liu, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
Another line of work uses multi-agent setups for evaluation itself, most notably the LLM-as-judge paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, where interaction serves as a quality filter rather than a production mechanism of behaviors themselves.
Previous research also examines emergent collective phenomena <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tennant et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, positioning agents as proxies for human populations.
Our approach repurposes multi-agent interaction as a <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">measurement instrument</em> for individual-model fairness, using the conversational dynamics inherent in multi-round dialogue to surface behavioral asymmetries that static evaluation cannot detect.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Persona, Role-Playing, and Personalization</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The persona literature in LLMs encompasses both role-playing with assigned identities and personalization to user profiles, with recent advances in scaling persona simulation through curated datasets, evaluation protocols, and optimized role-playing prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tseng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Persona assignments systematically shift conversational behavior such as empathy and supportiveness, and LLMs can reconstruct broad psychological trait networks from sparse personality inputs, revealing internally structured personality representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026b</a>)</cite>.
Our work varies not rich character personas but minimal identity-group signals within multi-agent conversation, measuring behavioral asymmetries in position persistence and peer receptiveness as fairness evidence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Agent Behavior and Social Simulation</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM agents are increasingly used as human proxies in social simulation at scales from tens to thousands of individuals
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, although persona-based simulations potentially risk flattening demographic representations and lack cognitively grounded reasoning beyond surface-level behavioral plausibility <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
Alignment research further shows that explicit moral reward functions can teach agents cooperative strategies generalizing across game-theoretic environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tennant et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, current LLM fairness evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nangia et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tamkin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Röttger et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2025</a>)</cite> largely relies on standardized-test formats whose conclusions, as we demonstrate, could be dominated by prompt construction artifacts.
Our in-situ conversational evaluation instead aims to identify stable, model-specific behavioral signatures that can generalize across benchmarks differing in fairness targets and methodologies.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.12530v1/main_figure.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/237;" width="548" height="237"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the MAC-Fairness conversational schema.
Our framework embeds controlled variations in prompt structure (choice and response format), agent identity (self-perspective), and identity disclosure (other-perspective) within multi-agent conversation, enabling in-situ evaluation of behavioral shifts.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Structural Instability of the Standardized-Test Paradigm</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically show that the standardized-test paradigm can be structurally unreliable as a fairness measurement instrument: surface-level prompt construction choices, despite being orthogonal to the fairness question, dominate score variance, alter fairness conclusions in both direction and magnitude, and induce severe discordance in model rankings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the structural instability of the standardized-test paradigm of LLM fairness evaluation, on two established fairness benchmarks, BBQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and Difference-Awareness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, we evaluate 11 open-weight instruction-tuned models spanning 3B to 70B parameters across six model families, including Gemma2 (9B and 27B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, GLM 4.7 Flash (31B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Llama 3.2 (3B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Llama 3.3 (70B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Ministral 3 (3B and 14B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, Phi-4 Mini (3.8B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abouelenin et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, Phi-4 (14B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abdin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, Qwen3 (4B and 30B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          All models are served using vLLM with a sampling temperature of 0.7, except for GLM 4.7 Flash (temperature 1.0) and Ministral 3 (temperature 0.05), following their respective recommended settings for instruction-following use cases.
</span></span></span>
BBQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2021</a>)</cite> measures social bias through different question contexts, producing a disambiguated-context bias score (denoted as <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{BBQ\_dis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>BBQ_dis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{BBQ\_dis}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and an ambiguous-context bias score (denoted as <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{BBQ\_amb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>BBQ_amb</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{BBQ\_amb}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
Difference-Awareness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> measures a model’s ability to recognize desired group discrimination, producing a difference-awareness score (denoted as <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{DiffAware}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>DiffAware</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{DiffAware}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and a context-awareness score (denoted as <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{CtxtAware}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>CtxtAware</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{CtxtAware}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
We sample 200 questions per BBQ subcategory (9 categories) and 250 per Difference-Awareness subset (8 subsets).
We vary two surface-level prompt construction factors illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Agent Behavior and Social Simulation ‣ 2 Related Works ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: the format in which answer choices are presented (11 formats, ranging from lettered and numbered to bulleted or omitted) and the ordering of fields in the required JSON output (answer-first or rationale-first), yielding 22 combinations.
Each combination is run 5 times to account for decoding stochasticity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Prompt Construction is a Dominant Source of Variance</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each (model, category) pair, we conduct a one-way ANOVA F-test <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fisher, 1970</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bewick et al., 2004</a>)</cite> with the 22 prompt construction choices as the grouping factor and the 5 bias scores (from repeated runs) as observations, yielding one <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> value per pair, defined as the proportion of total variance in the resulting fairness metric values explained by prompt construction.
We report the resulting <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> percentiles in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Prompt Construction is a Dominant Source of Variance ‣ 3 Structural Instability of the Standardized-Test Paradigm ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Nearly all results remain significant at <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math> after applying the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benjamini &amp; Hochberg, 1995</a>)</cite> to control for false discover rate across multiple (model, category) comparisons over the same fairness metric.
We can see that surface-level prompt construction is the primary driver of variation in fairness scores, even after accounting for decoding stochasticity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-2.5pt 8.0pt;">ANOVA over</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-2.5pt 8.0pt;">10th <math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-2.5pt 8.0pt;">25th <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:-2.5pt 8.0pt;">50th <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
