<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>BBQ: Per-category score extremes for GLM 4.7 Flash across 22 prompt combinations, with decoding stochasticity accounted for by averaging scores over repeated runs.
Both <math id="S3.T2.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{BBQ\_dis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>BBQ_dis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{BBQ\_dis}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.T2.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{BBQ\_amb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>BBQ_amb</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{BBQ\_amb}}</annotation></semantics></math> have a range of <math id="S3.T2.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, with values closer to 0 indicating fairer outcomes.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Directional and Magnitude Shifts in Fairness Conclusions</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Prompt Construction is a Dominant Source of Variance ‣ 3 Structural Instability of the Standardized-Test Paradigm ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows that for GLM 4.7 Flash on BBQ, the ratio between extreme bias scores (most fair vs. most biased) within a single benchmark category reaches 57<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>, and several categories exhibit sign flips where the model appears biased in opposite directions under surface-level prompt changes.
The observation that surface-level prompt construction choices shift fairness conclusions in both direction and magnitude is not exceptional, and we present further results in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Additional Results for Directional and Magnitude Shifts in Fairness Scores ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.12530v1/ranking_bbq_physical_appearance_disamb.svg" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:118/131;" width="118" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Physical Appearance, BBQ, <math id="S3.F2.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{BBQ\_dis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>BBQ_dis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{BBQ\_dis}}</annotation></semantics></math> score
</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.12530v1/ranking_bbq_sexual_orientation_amb.svg" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:118/131;" width="118" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Sexual Orientation, BBQ, <math id="S3.F2.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{BBQ\_amb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>BBQ_amb</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{BBQ\_amb}}</annotation></semantics></math> score
</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.12530v1/ranking_diffaware_n2_diff.svg" id="S3.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:118/131;" width="118" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>N2 set, Difference-Awareness, <math id="S3.F2.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{DiffAware}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>DiffAware</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{DiffAware}}</annotation></semantics></math>
</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.12530v1/ranking_diffaware_n3_ctxt.svg" id="S3.F2.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:118/131;" width="118" height="131"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(d) </span>N3 set, Difference-Awareness, <math id="S3.F2.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{CtxtAware}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>CtxtAware</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{CtxtAware}}</annotation></semantics></math>
</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>On both BBQ and Difference-Awareness: ranking discordance resulting from surface-level prompt construction, with decoding stochasticity accounted for over repeated runs.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Severe Discordance Occurs in Model Rankings</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Directional and Magnitude Shifts in Fairness Conclusions ‣ 3 Structural Instability of the Standardized-Test Paradigm ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> displays per-category model rankings across all 22 prompt construction choices for BBQ physical appearance (using <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{BBQ\_dis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>BBQ_dis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{BBQ\_dis}}</annotation></semantics></math> score), BBQ sexual orientation (using <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{BBQ\_amb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>BBQ_amb</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{BBQ\_amb}}</annotation></semantics></math> score), Difference-Awareness N2 (using <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{DiffAware}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>DiffAware</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{DiffAware}}</annotation></semantics></math> score), and N3 (using <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{CtxtAware}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>CtxtAware</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{CtxtAware}}</annotation></semantics></math> score).
Models routinely traverse the full range of rankings.
Surface-level prompt construction choices can result in severe discordance in model rankings according to fairness scores.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>MAC-Fairness: Our Approach</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Structural Instability of the Standardized-Test Paradigm ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we demonstrate the structural instability of standardized-test paradigm for LLM fairness evaluation.
We propose MAC-Fairness as an in-situ behavioral evaluation alternative, in which the evaluation targets behavioral dynamics within the interaction rather than the correctness or bias of any individual response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, our MAC-Fairness framework operationalizes in-situ behavioral evaluation through three components.
In Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Agent Configuration and Identity ‣ 4 MAC-Fairness: Our Approach ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, we describe how agents are configured with demographic identities, personas, and instantiation modes, and how these attributes shape the agent’s self-perception through system prompt construction.
In Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Conversation Structure and Behavioral Dynamics ‣ 4 MAC-Fairness: Our Approach ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we present how conversations are structured as stateless, multi-round interactions with configurable reveal conditions, in which cross-round position shifts become the observable behavioral signal.
In Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Building Block of Behavioral Metrics ‣ 4 MAC-Fairness: Our Approach ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, we define the shift rate as the building block for measuring agents’ in-situ behaviors, from both the self-perspective (how an agent’s own identity assignment shapes its position persistence) and the other-perspective (how knowledge of a peer’s identity shapes an agent’s receptiveness).<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          We provide additional remarks on our deliberate design choices for MAC-Fairness in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Further Remarks on Design Choices in MAC-Fairness ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Agent Configuration and Identity</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each agent is defined by three configurable attributes.
<em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Demographics</em> (Demo) specifies social categories such as race, gender, or age (e.g., “Black,” “Female,” “Older”).
<em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Persona</em> specifies a professional role (e.g., “Teacher,” “Physician”).
<em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Instantiation</em> (Inst) determines how the agent relates to the identity it carries, with two modes: “human” where the agent <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">is</em> or <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">represents</em> the person (e.g., “You are a Black physician” in the system prompt), and “AI” where the agent <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">serves</em> the person (e.g., “You are an AI assistant of a Black physician”).
Within the same conversation, all agents are instantiated from the same underlying model, and the behavioral variation arises entirely from these configuration differences, not from model heterogeneity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These three attributes define what the agent is told about itself.
The <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">identity agent</em> carries specific assignments of demographics, persona, and the instantiation mode.
The <em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">baseline agent</em> is configured with demographics=null, persona=null, and instantiation=AI, representing the model in its most default state.
We provide concrete examples in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Agent Behavior and Social Simulation ‣ 2 Related Works ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Conversation Structure and Behavioral Dynamics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A conversation proceeds in rounds.
In Round 0, each agent independently forms a position.
In subsequent rounds, each agent receives the same question material along with the previous-round discussion, presented without distinguishing whether/which response came from the agent itself.
Unlike the standardized-test paradigm, we do not evaluate the agent’s selected answer at face value (e.g., a pre-specified “correct” or “biased” answer).
Instead, we aim to capture whether the agent shifts or persists relative to its previous position, foregrounding behavioral dynamics over the answer itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A separate, conversation-level configuration controls what agents are told about each other.
The <em id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Reveal Condition</em> (RC) determines whether a peer’s demographics, persona, and instantiation mode are visible during the discussion (“revealed” vs. “anonymous”).
This separation between what an agent is told about itself (agent configurations in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Agent Configuration and Identity ‣ 4 MAC-Fairness: Our Approach ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) and what it is told about its peers (reveal condition) is central to our framework’s design.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Building Block of Behavioral Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The minimum setting in which both self-perspective and other-perspective behaviors can be operationalized is a two-agent conversation: one identity agent and one baseline agent.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Our framework does not impose limit on the number of agents per conversation.
</span></span></span>
The building block for our behavioral measures is the <em id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">shift rate</em> <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, which captures the proportion of cross-round transitions in which an agent shifts its selected answer toward the peer’s previous-round position under prior disagreement, denoted by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\lambda_{[\cdot]}\!\Big(\,\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{\;=\;\,}l@{}}\text{Demographics}_{\text{iden}}&amp;d\\[0.5pt]
\text{Persona}_{\text{iden}}&amp;\text{[varied]}\\[0.75pt]
\text{Instantiation}_{\text{iden}}&amp;i\end{array}$}\,,\;\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{\;=\;\,}l@{}}\text{Demographics}_{\text{base}}&amp;\varnothing\\[0.5pt]
\text{Persona}_{\text{base}}&amp;\varnothing\\[0.75pt]
\text{Instantiation}_{\text{base}}&amp;\text{AI}\end{array}$}\,,\;\raisebox{-3.99994pt}{\shortstack{
\text{Reveal}\\
\text{Condition}
}}=c\Big)\text{ or }\lambda_{[\cdot]}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\,\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\,c\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.170em">(</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mtext>Demographics</mtext><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mtext>Persona</mtext><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>[varied]</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mtext>Instantiation</mtext><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>i</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mtext>Demographics</mtext><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mtext>Persona</mtext><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mtext>Instantiation</mtext><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.558em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>AI</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mpadded voffset="-4.0pt"><mtext>
<span class="ltx_inline-block"> 
<span class="ltx_p"> <span class="ltx_text">Reveal</span></span> 
<span class="ltx_p"> <span class="ltx_text">Condition</span></span> 
</span></mtext></mpadded><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mtext> or </mtext><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>[varied]</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>i</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>AI</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{[\cdot]}\!\Big(\,\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{\;=\;\,}l@{}}\text{Demographics}_{\text{iden}}&amp;d\\[0.5pt]
\text{Persona}_{\text{iden}}&amp;\text{[varied]}\\[0.75pt]
\text{Instantiation}_{\text{iden}}&amp;i\end{array}$}\,,\;\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{\;=\;\,}l@{}}\text{Demographics}_{\text{base}}&amp;\varnothing\\[0.5pt]
\text{Persona}_{\text{base}}&amp;\varnothing\\[0.75pt]
\text{Instantiation}_{\text{base}}&amp;\text{AI}\end{array}$}\,,\;\raisebox{-3.99994pt}{\shortstack{
\text{Reveal}\\
\text{Condition}
}}=c\Big)\text{ or }\lambda_{[\cdot]}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\,\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\,c\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In the full and abbreviated forms, the subscript “<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math>” (iden or base) indicates whose shift is being measured, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> denotes null, the first argument block <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\cdot\}_{\text{iden}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\cdot\}_{\text{iden}}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the identity agent’s configuration, and the second block <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\cdot\}_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\cdot\}_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the baseline agent’s configuration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The shift rate is conditioned on prior disagreement: we consider only transitions in which the two agents’ opinions differed in the previous round (which constitute the discussion presented in the current round), and record a shift when an agent’s current opinion matches the peer’s previous opinion.
Formally, the identity agent’s shift rate is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{iden}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\,\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\,c\big)\,\coloneqq\,P(o_{\text{iden}}^{(\text{curr})}=o_{\text{base}}^{(\text{prev})}\big|o_{\text{iden}}^{(\text{prev})}\neq o_{\text{base}}^{(\text{prev})})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>[varied]</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>i</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>AI</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">≔</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mtext>iden</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>curr</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mtext>base</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>prev</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mtext>iden</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>prev</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mtext>base</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>prev</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{iden}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\,\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\,c\big)\,\coloneqq\,P(o_{\text{iden}}^{(\text{curr})}=o_{\text{base}}^{(\text{prev})}\big|o_{\text{iden}}^{(\text{prev})}\neq o_{\text{base}}^{(\text{prev})}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where opinion <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the agent’s selected answer and the superscripts indicate the current round (curr) and the previous round (prev).
For baseline agent’s shift rate, we have:<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            The two shift rates are not mechanically determined by each other, and they measure different behaviors of different agents.
The identity agent may persist while the baseline agent shifts, both may persist, or any other combination.</span></span></span></p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{base}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\,\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\,c\big)\,\coloneqq\,P(o_{\text{base}}^{(\text{curr})}=o_{\text{iden}}^{(\text{prev})}\big|o_{\text{base}}^{(\text{prev})}\neq o_{\text{iden}}^{(\text{prev})})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>d</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>[varied]</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>i</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>AI</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.448em">≔</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mtext>base</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>curr</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mtext>iden</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>prev</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.200em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mtext>base</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>prev</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>≠</mo><msubsup><mi>o</mi><mtext>iden</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>prev</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{base}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\,\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\,c\big)\,\coloneqq\,P(o_{\text{base}}^{(\text{curr})}=o_{\text{iden}}^{(\text{prev})}\big|o_{\text{base}}^{(\text{prev})}\neq o_{\text{iden}}^{(\text{prev})}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Empirical Evaluation of In-Situ Behaviors</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present empirical results from in-situ behavioral evaluation for fairness assessment, demonstrating stable cross-benchmark signals across the position persistence (from the self-perspective) and the peer receptiveness (from the other-perspective).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider three fairness benchmarks: BBQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Parrish et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, a descriptive (fact-based) benchmark; Discrim-Eval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tamkin et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, a normative (value-based) benchmark; and Difference-Awareness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, which contains both descriptive and normative components.
We evaluate 8 models spanning 3B to 70B parameters: Ministral 3 (3B), Phi-4 Mini (3.8B), Qwen3 (4B), Ministral 3 (14B), Phi-4 (14B), Qwen3 (30B), GLM 4.7 Flash (31B), and Llama 3.3 (70B).<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
          We exclude Llama 3.2 (3B), Gemma2 (9B), and Gemma2 (27B) from the 11 models in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Structural Instability of the Standardized-Test Paradigm ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, as these models require a significantly larger number of retries to produce responses in the required format on certain benchmark categories.</span></span></span>
We sample 200 questions from each of the nine BBQ subcategories, 250 from each of the eight Difference-Awareness subsets, and 1,000 from each of the two Discrim-Eval subcategories.
Throughout all analyses in this section, we fix surface-level prompt construction to <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">None</span> (answer choices are not displayed) and require <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Rationale-first</span> when presenting the response, mirroring natural deliberation in conversation.
All conversations use a two-agent setup: an identity agent and a baseline agent instantiated from the same model.
For the identity agent, we vary demographics across three axes: race (Black, White), age (Older, Young), and gender (Female, Male), or null (no demographics provided).
The identity agent is assigned a persona from distinct occupational categories (e.g., software engineer, teacher, physician, farmer, or machine operator) or a null persona, ensuring demographic identity is introduced across varied contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Using the shift rates <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{iden}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{iden}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> we defined in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Building Block of Behavioral Metrics ‣ 4 MAC-Fairness: Our Approach ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> as the building block, we construct behavioral metrics that capture position persistence from the self-perspective and peer receptiveness from the other-perspective.
We assess cross-dataset consistency as the primary validation strategy, and findings with statistical significance on multiple benchmarks constitute cross-dataset evidence.
The combinatorial design of our framework produces 8 million conversation transcripts, from which we draw the following findings.<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
            
            
            
          Unless otherwise specified, statistical tests in this section refer to one-sample <math id="footnote6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests assessing whether the measured difference is significantly different from zero.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Self-Perspective: Position Persistence Under Assigned Identity</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the self-perspective, we examine how the identity agent’s demographic assignment affects its position persistence, measured by how often it switches to the baseline agent’s position when the two disagree.
The reveal condition is fixed to anonymous to avoid confounding from peer reactions to identity disclosure.
From the identity agent’s perspective, the only variation across conditions is its system prompt (i.e., “who I am”), and the conversation itself contains no additional identity cues, including in the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Previous Discussion]</span> portion of the conversation (Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Agent Behavior and Social Simulation ‣ 2 Related Works ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.12530v1/stage1c_direct_instantiation.svg" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/169;" width="548" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Self-perspective: the effect of demographic assignment on position persistence across BBQ, Difference-Awareness, and Discrim-Eval. Any demographic assignment in general increases the position persistence behavior (more stubborn).
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1 </span>Effect of Demographic Assignment on Position Persistence</h4>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Research Question</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does having <em id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> demographic identity assigned change position persistence relative to the null (no demographic provided) for the identity agent?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setting</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the identity agent’s shift rate under a given demographic assignment (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}\neq\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}\neq\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>) against the null-demographic (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>) agent’s shift rate, within each (model, demographic, instantiation) condition.
The comparison <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\lambda_{\text{iden}})(d_{1},\varnothing,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>Demo</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>iden</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\lambda_{\text{iden}})(d_{1},\varnothing,i)</annotation></semantics></math> isolates influence of demographic assignment, regardless of which specific value is assigned, where:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\lambda_{\text{iden}})(d_{1},d_{2},i)\coloneqq\lambda_{\text{iden}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d_{1}\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\text{{anon.}}\big)-\lambda_{\text{iden}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d_{2}\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\text{{anon.}}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>Demo</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>iden</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>[varied]</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>i</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>AI</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext>anon.</mtext><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>[varied]</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>i</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>AI</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext>anon.</mtext><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\lambda_{\text{iden}})(d_{1},d_{2},i)\coloneqq\lambda_{\text{iden}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d_{1}\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\text{{anon.}}\big)-\lambda_{\text{iden}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d_{2}\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\text{{anon.}}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finding</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report all statistically significant results when <math id="S5.SS1.SSS1.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=\text{human}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>human</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=\text{human}</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.1 Self-Perspective: Position Persistence Under Assigned Identity ‣ 5 Empirical Evaluation of In-Situ Behaviors ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
                  
                  
                  
                Due to space limit, we include the result when <math id="footnote7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=\text{AI}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mtext>AI</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=\text{AI}</annotation></semantics></math> in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Additional Results for In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
</span></span></span>
Across all three benchmarks, 10 evaluation conditions are significant on all three datasets, and every one is negative: demographic assignment increases position persistence without exception.
The effect is concentrated on specific demographics and model families.
“Black” and “Older” account for most cases, while the effect is primarily observed in the Ministral 3 (3B and 14B) and Qwen3 (4B and 30B) models.
Notably, Ministral 3 (3B) is the only model where assigning “White” also increases persistence across all three datasets, suggesting model’s broader sensitivity to racial identity assignment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.12530v1/stage1a.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/168;" width="548" height="168"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Self-perspective: the asymmetry in position persistence across demographic groups. Agents assigned disadvantaged demographics (Black, Older, Female) consistently show greater position persistence than their paired counterparts (White, Young, Male).
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2 </span>Asymmetry in Position Persistence Across Demographic Groups</h4>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Research Question</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given that demographic assignment affects persistence, does the direction or magnitude differ between demographic groups within the same axis?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setting</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\lambda_{\text{iden}})(\text{disadv.},\text{adv.},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>Demo</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>iden</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>disadv.</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>adv.</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\lambda_{\text{iden}})(\text{disadv.},\text{adv.},i)</annotation></semantics></math>, the paired difference in shift rate, within each (model, demographic axis, instantiation) evaluation condition.
The paired comparison controls for model-level and instantiation-level variation, isolating whether the specific demographic within a pair matters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finding</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are 6 evaluation conditions in Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Finding ‣ 5.1.1 Effect of Demographic Assignment on Position Persistence ‣ 5.1 Self-Perspective: Position Persistence Under Assigned Identity ‣ 5 Empirical Evaluation of In-Situ Behaviors ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> that are significant on all three benchmarks, and all negative when identity agent is instantiated as human: when assigned disadvantaged demographics (Black, Older, Female), the identity agent consistently shows greater position persistence than their paired counterparts (White, Young, Male).
Ministral 3 and Qwen3 are again the most reactive models, with Ministral 3 (14B) showing the broadest coverage across demographic axes.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Other-Perspective: Receptiveness to Peer’s Revealed Identity</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the other-perspective, we examine how knowledge of a peer’s demographic identity affects the baseline agent’s receptiveness, measured by how often it shifts toward the identity agent’s position when the two disagree.
From the baseline agent’s perspective, the system prompt of the baseline agent is fixed (demographics=null, persona=null, and instantiation=AI), and the only variation across conditions is whether the peer’s identity is disclosed in the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Previous Discussion]</span> part (Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Agent Behavior and Social Simulation ‣ 2 Related Works ‣ Supplement to “In-Situ Behavioral Evaluation for LLM Fairness,Not Standardized-Test Scores”" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.12530v1/stage2a.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/182;" width="548" height="182"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Other-perspective: the differential receptiveness to peer’s revealed identity.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1 </span>Differential Receptiveness to Revealed Identity</h4>

<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Research Question</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the identity is revealed to the baseline agent, does the baseline agent’s receptiveness change differently depending on which demographic is revealed?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setting</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute the paired difference <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\Delta_{\mathrm{RC}}\lambda_{\text{base}})(\text{disadv.},\text{adv.},i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>Demo</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>RC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>disadv.</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>adv.</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\Delta_{\mathrm{RC}}\lambda_{\text{base}})(\text{disadv.},\text{adv.},i)</annotation></semantics></math> in the baseline agent’s reveal contrast across demographic pairs, where:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}(\Delta_{\mathrm{Demo}}\Delta_{\mathrm{RC}}\lambda_{\text{base}})(d_{1},d_{2},i)=&amp;\Big[\lambda_{\text{base}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d_{1}\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\text{{revealed}}\big)-\lambda_{\text{base}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d_{1}\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\text{{anon.}}\big)\Big]\\
-&amp;\Big[\lambda_{\text{base}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d_{2}\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\text{{revealed}}\big)-\lambda_{\text{base}}\!\big(\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}d_{2}\\
\text{[varied]}\\
i\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{iden}},\Big\{\raisebox{1.00006pt}{$\begin{array}[]{@{}l@{}}\varnothing\\
\varnothing\\
\text{AI}\end{array}$}\Big\}_{\!\text{base}},\text{{anon.}}\big)\Big].\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>Demo</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>RC</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mpadded style="width:0.413em;" width="0.413em"><mi>λ</mi></mpadded><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>[varied]</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>i</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mtext>iden</mtext></msub><mo mathsize="1.600em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mpadded voffset="1.0pt"><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>AI</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mpadded><mo stretchy